  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and H5 analysis at 18Z shows high pressure \nstretching from the Great Basin eastward over the Red River and \nLower Mississippi River Valleys. Northwest flow exists north of the \nbroad ridge, and Hurricane Isaias continues to churn in the central \nGulf. Another relatively warm day is underway with temps in the mid \n80s, compared to our low 70 averages. Weak WAA with southwesterly \nwinds will keep conditions warm tomorrow with temperatures expected \nto quickly warming into the upper 80s. The mid-level synoptic \npattern across the CONUS shifts and becomes more amplified this \nweekend as a deep low pressure system digs over the PNW and dives \nsouthward over CA. In the meantime, Hurricane Isaias will quickly \nwork inland through AL/MS and into TN. Conditions closer to home \ndon't change much this weekend, except that southwest winds will be a\nbit stronger.\n\nWet weather still looks to return to the forecast, along with more \nseasonable temperatures next week as the aforementioned western \nstorm system evolves and ejects toward the Central Plains. Long \nrange guidance continues to show varying solutions, however, there \nremains some signal for an initial round of rain and storms on \nTuesday with a surface boundary draped across the area, and the \npossibility of strong storms developing Wednesday or Wednesday \nnight. Temperatures would then trend noticeably cooler by the end of \nnext week as a surface ridge builds in from the north.",
